From 677f9d896ce5f9ef99aa59b931257b9d36fd1daa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:06:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] v3.0.4: hold the revived self-heals off until the box is past
 early boot

FIELD FAILURE, v3.0.3 (owner, 2026-09-01): flashing froze the router, it needed a
manual reboot, it did not come online, and Wi-Fi never started. Ethernet DID
work, which is the detail that narrows it: the box booted, the LAN bridge came
up, rc got that far - then the WAN never completed and the radios never started.
Rolled back to v3.0.0, which is fine.

Cause not proven. This change removes the most suspicious thing v3.0.1
introduced, on the reasoning below, without giving up the fix that rung existed
for.

WHAT v3.0.1 ACTUALLY CHANGED, beyond the stated fix: making `reaper_lockrun`
dispatchable revived THREE self-heals that had been silent no-ops since v2.7.3 -
14 months of code that had never once executed in the field. Two of them run
BEFORE the boot grace deliberately (rwatch.c: "boot is when it happens"), and
each takes file_lock("firewall") and runs a chain-rebuilding script. v3.0.1 also
put reaper_pbr_reassert() into wan_up(), which wants that SAME lock while the WAN
is coming up. So the rung created two new firewall-lock-taking, script-executing
paths during boot where there had been none, and the symptom shape - LAN up, WAN
never completes, radios never start, boot looks frozen - is what contention or a
stall in that window would look like.

That is exactly the class the wifi-accelerator rule warns about: dormant periodic
code enabled wholesale without field lineage. It was described as a pure win in
the v3.0.1 message; reviving it was a behavioural change and deserved its own
validation.

THE FIX: gate all three heals behind HEALGRACE (300s uptime). They still work -
this only stops them firing in the fragile early-boot window, which also closes
the lock-contention window against the wan_up() re-assert. The PBR boot heal is
redundant now anyway: v3.0.1 fixes the fail-open at its source by re-asserting
after add_multi_routes(), so the heal is defence-in-depth, not the mechanism.
rwatch_enable=0 remains the full kill switch.

KEPT, deliberately: the IPv4 fail-open fix (the reason v3.0.1 exists - marks set
with no ip rule to select on, traffic leaving unencrypted), the Warden
drops-viewer prefix fix, OpenSSL 3.5.8, and the v3.0.3 speed-test work.

ALSO FOUND while investigating, real but NOT the cause: hostapd and
wpa_supplicant-2.7 ship with 141/236 unresolved OpenSSL symbols, because they
were never rebuilt after the 1.1 -> shim swap and the shim exports only 143.
They are harmless here - RTCONFIG_BRCM_HOSTAPD is unset, they are never built or
launched, and Wi-Fi auth runs through eapd/nas which link no OpenSSL. Left alone
in this rung; the new check_symbols.sh gate reports them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 16c879a7d63c151ce93e804cc428fdee9f8cc810)
---
 release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c | 19 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c b/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c
index fb57a5aefc..da59f945ed 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c
@@ -85,6 +85,16 @@ static void rwatch_gen(void)
 	  "case \"$DS\" in *,watch,*) [ \"$VOLATILE\" = 0 ] && DUMPROOT=\"$BASE/rwatch\";; esac\n"
 	  "\n"
 	  "UP=$(cut -d. -f1 /proc/uptime)\n"
+	  /* reaper v3.0.4: HEAL BOOT GRACE. Until v3.0.1 the three heals below
+	   * were silent no-ops - `rc reaper_lockrun` never dispatched (see
+	   * rc-applet-dispatch). Making them real revived 14 months of untested
+	   * code that takes file_lock("firewall") and rebuilds chains, and two of
+	   * them ran BEFORE the boot grace on purpose. That collides with the
+	   * reaper_pbr_reassert() v3.0.1 added to wan_up(), which wants the same
+	   * lock while the WAN is still coming up. Hold them off until the box is
+	   * past early boot; the fail-open they guarded is fixed at its source
+	   * now, so a boot-time heal is no longer the mechanism that matters. */
+	  "HEALGRACE=300\n"
 	  /* reaper v2.6.1: reap `nvram` readers stuck in the libnvram netlink
 	   * wait-forever (see reaper_nv.h). _nv guards OUR scripts; this catches
 	   * every other caller (stock cron jobs, httpd children, ad-hoc shells).
@@ -126,7 +136,8 @@ static void rwatch_gen(void)
 	   * load when its ipset is not there yet at the instant apply.sh runs.
 	   * apply.sh records how many mark rules it meant to load (expect); when
 	   * the live chain is short, re-run it (idempotent) - once per 2 minutes,
-	   * logged. Runs before the boot grace on purpose: boot is when it happens. */
+	   * logged. Held off until HEALGRACE (v3.0.4): the boot case is now covered
+	   * at source by reaper_pbr_reassert() after add_multi_routes(). */
 	  "if [ \"$(_nv reaper_pbr_active)\" = 1 ] && [ -s /tmp/reaper_pbr/expect ]; then\n"
 	  "  _ex=$(cat /tmp/reaper_pbr/expect 2>/dev/null); case \"$_ex\" in ''|*[!0-9]*) _ex=0;; esac\n"
 	  "  _sh=/tmp/reaper_pbr/apply.sh; [ -d /tmp/reaper_pbr/armed ] && [ -f /tmp/reaper_pbr/candidate.sh ] && _sh=/tmp/reaper_pbr/candidate.sh\n"
@@ -145,7 +156,7 @@ static void rwatch_gen(void)
 	  "  if [ \"$_ex\" -gt 0 ] && [ \"$_live\" -lt \"$_ex\" ] && [ \"$_n\" -ge 5 ]; then\n"
 	  "    [ -f /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.gaveup ] || { : > /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.gaveup; logger -t rwatch \"policy routing: $_live of $_ex mark rule(s) live after 5 heals - giving up until it converges or you re-apply\"; }\n"
 	  "  fi\n"
-	  "  if [ \"$_ex\" -gt 0 ] && [ \"$_live\" -lt \"$_ex\" ] && [ \"$_n\" -lt 5 ] && [ $((UP-_last)) -ge 120 ] && [ -x \"$_sh\" ]; then\n"
+	  "  if [ \"$UP\" -ge \"$HEALGRACE\" ] && [ \"$_ex\" -gt 0 ] && [ \"$_live\" -lt \"$_ex\" ] && [ \"$_n\" -lt 5 ] && [ $((UP-_last)) -ge 120 ] && [ -x \"$_sh\" ]; then\n"
 	  "    echo $UP > /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.at; echo $((_n+1)) > /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.n\n"
 	  "    logger -t rwatch \"policy routing: $_live of $_ex mark rule(s) live - re-applying\"\n"
 	  /* v2.7.3 (code-review P3): under rc's firewall lock. shell flock(1)
@@ -166,7 +177,7 @@ static void rwatch_gen(void)
 	  "  if [ \"$_exr\" -gt 0 ] && [ \"$_lvr\" -lt \"$_exr\" ] && [ \"$_nr\" -ge 5 ]; then\n"
 	  "    [ -f /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.rgaveup ] || { : > /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.rgaveup; logger -t rwatch \"policy routing: $_lvr of $_exr ip rule(s) live after 5 heals - giving up until it converges or you re-apply\"; }\n"
 	  "  fi\n"
-	  "  if [ \"$_exr\" -gt 0 ] && [ \"$_lvr\" -lt \"$_exr\" ] && [ \"$_nr\" -lt 5 ] && [ $((UP-_lstr)) -ge 120 ] && [ -x \"$_shr\" ]; then\n"
+	  "  if [ \"$UP\" -ge \"$HEALGRACE\" ] && [ \"$_exr\" -gt 0 ] && [ \"$_lvr\" -lt \"$_exr\" ] && [ \"$_nr\" -lt 5 ] && [ $((UP-_lstr)) -ge 120 ] && [ -x \"$_shr\" ]; then\n"
 	  "    echo $UP > /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.rat; echo $((_nr+1)) > /tmp/reaper_pbr/heal.rn\n"
 	  "    logger -t rwatch \"policy routing: $_lvr of $_exr ip rule(s) live - re-applying\"\n"
 	  "    reaper_lockrun \"$_shr\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n"
@@ -290,7 +301,7 @@ static void rwatch_gen(void)
 	  "if [ -n \"$CRIT\" ]; then\n"
 	  "  logger -t rwatch \"CRITICAL:$CRIT - LAN/loopback found in a Warden block set (ingest filter breached); re-applied warden chain\"\n"
 	  /* v2.7.3: same firewall-lock discipline as the PBR heal above */
-	  "  [ -f /tmp/rwarden/apply.sh ] && reaper_lockrun /tmp/rwarden/apply.sh >/dev/null 2>&1\n"
+	  "  [ \"$UP\" -ge \"$HEALGRACE\" ] && [ -f /tmp/rwarden/apply.sh ] && reaper_lockrun /tmp/rwarden/apply.sh >/dev/null 2>&1\n"
 	  "fi\n"
 	  "\n"
 	  "if [ -n \"$FAIL$CRIT\" ]; then\n"
-- 
2.25.1

